ja de Santa Rita de Cássia it is a Catholic temple of historical and artistic importance in the city of Rio de Janeiro, Brazil, listed by National Institute of Historic and Artistic Heritage (IPHAN) in 1938.","wpu":"Igreja de Santa Rita de Cássia (Rio de Janeiro)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f8/Igreja_de_Santa_Rita_de_C%C3%A1ssia_-_Rio_de_Janeiro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Ilha das Cobras","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-22.895,"lo":-43.172222,"d":1104,"ds":"Ilha das Cobras (Portuguese for 'Isle of the Snakes') is an island located within Guanabara Bay in the city and state of Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. It is east of the neighborhood Guanabara. It is home to the Arsenal de Marinha do Rio de Janeiro base of the Brazilian Navy.","wpu":"Ilha das Cobras","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/IlhaCobras.jpg/960px-IlhaCobras.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Royal Portuguese Cabinet of Reading","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-22.9053,"lo":-43.1822,"d":1148,"ds":"The Royal Portuguese Cabinet of Reading (Portuguese: Real Gabinete Português de Leitura) is a library and lusophone cultural institution, is located in Luís de Camões Street, number 30, in the center of the city of Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. It is listed by the State Institute of Cultural Heritage.","wpu":"Royal Portuguese Cabinet of Reading","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Real_Gabinete_Portugu%C3%AAs_de_Leitura_07.jpg/960px-Real_Gabinete_Portugu%C3%AAs_de_Leitura_07.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Old Cathedral of Rio de Janeiro","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-22.903333,"lo":-43.175556,"d":1200,"ds":"The Old Cathedral of Rio de Janeiro dedicated to Our Lady of Mount Carmel (full name in Portuguese: Igreja de Nossa Senhora do Monte do Carmo da antiga Sé, lit. 'Church of Our Lady of Mount Carmel of the Ancient See') is an old Carmelite church which served as cathedral (Sé) of Rio de Janeiro from around 1808 until 1976.","wpu":"Old Cathedral of Rio de Janeiro","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Igreja_Nossa_Senhora_do_Carmo%2C_Rio_de_Janeiro_-_RJ_-_panoramio.jpg/960px-Igreja_Nossa_Senhora_do_Carmo%2C_Rio_de_Janeiro_-_RJ_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Paço Imperial","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-22.903508,"lo":-43.174233,"d":1304,"ds":"The Paço Imperial (Brazilian Portuguese: [ˈpasu ĩpeɾiˈaw]), or Imperial Palace, previously known as the Royal Palace of Rio de Janeiro and Palace of the Viceroys, is a historic building in the center of the city of Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. The Paço Imperial was built in the 18th century to serve as a residence for the governors of colonial Brazil.","wpu":"Paço Imperial","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Pa%C3%A7o_Imperial_-_Rio_de_Janeiro_-_20220826172010_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-Pa%C3%A7o_Imperial_-_Rio_de_Janeiro_-_20220826172010_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Teatro Carlos Gomes","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-22.9075,"lo":-43.1826,"d":1390,"ds":"Teatro Carlos Gomes is a theatre in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. It was established in 1868 in the Hotel Richelieu. Teatro Carlos Gomes is a theater located in Praça Tiradentes, in the center of the city of Rio de Janeiro.","wpu":"Teatro Carlos Gomes","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/TeatroCarlosGomes.jpg/960px-TeatroCarlosGomes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Campo de Santana (park)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-22.906667,"lo":-43.188611,"d":1418,"ds":"The Campo de Santana is a park located in the Praça da República in downtown Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. The name of the plaza refers to the fact that it is located near the site where Brazil was declared a republic in 1889.","wpu":"Campo de Santana (park)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Arquitetura_externa_campo_de_santana.jpg/960px-Arquitetura_externa_campo_de_santana.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Theatro Lyrico Fluminense","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-22.90685,"lo":-43.189861,"d":1493,"ds":"The Theatro Lyrico Fluminense (also spelled as Teatro Lírico Fluminense) was one of the main theatres in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil, during the mid-19th century and was for many years the primary theatre for opera performances in that city. It was demolished on 30 April 1875 to make way for a park.","wpu":"Theatro Lyrico Fluminense","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/75/Teatro_Provisorio_Rio_de_Janeiro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Pedro Ernesto Palace","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-22.91,"lo":-43.18,"d":1695,"ds":"The Pedro Ernesto Palace is a historic building located in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. Since 1923, the building has housed the Municipal Chamber of Rio de Janeiro.","wpu":"Pedro Ernesto Palace","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Pal%C3%A1cio_Pedro_Ernesto_na_Cinel%C3%A2ndia.jpg/960px-Pal%C3%A1cio_Pedro_Ernesto_na_Cinel%C3%A2ndia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Ilha Fiscal","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-22.896944,"lo":-43.166389,"d":1715,"ds":"Ilha Fiscal, or Fiscal Island, is an island in Guanabara Bay, bordering the historic city center of Rio de Janeiro, in southeastern Brazil. Originally named by Europeans as Rat Island (Portuguese: Ilha dos Ratos), its current name comes from the fact that the customs department has been stationed there before Guarda Fiscal, serving the port of the then-capital of the Empire during the nineteenth century.","wpu":"Ilha Fiscal","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Ilha_Fiscal_no_Rio_de_Janeiro_%2853299621447%29.jpg/960px-Ilha_Fiscal_no_Rio_de_Janeiro_%2853299621447%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Theatro Municipal (Rio de Janeiro)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-22.909222,"lo":-43.176492,"d":1716,"ds":"The Theatro Municipal (\"Municipal Theater\") is an opera house in the Centro district of Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. Built in the early twentieth century, it is considered to be one of the most beautiful and important theaters in the country. The building is designed in an eclectic style, inspired by the Paris Opéra of Charles Garnier.","wpu":"Theatro Municipal (Rio de Janeiro)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Brazil-00988_-_Rio_de_Janeiro_Municipal_Theatre_%2848984301008%29.jpg/960px-Brazil-00988_-_Rio_de_Janeiro_Municipal_Theatre_%2848984301008%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Cemitério dos Ingleses, Gamboa","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-22.9,"lo":-43.2,"d":1827,"ds":"The Cemitério dos Ingleses, Gamboa is a cemetery in Gamboa, Rio de Janeiro in Brazil. It is also known as the English Cemetery or the British Cemetery Gamboa.","wpu":"Cemitério dos Ingleses, Gamboa","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/British_Burial_Ground_-_Cemit%C3%A9rio_dos_Ingleses_na_Gamboa_01.jpg/960px-British_Burial_Ground_-_Cemit%C3%A9rio_dos_Ingleses_na_Gamboa_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"National Historical Museum (Brazil)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-22.905908,"lo":-43.169522,"d":1837,"ds":"The National Historical Museum (Portuguese: Museu Histórico Nacional) of Brazil was created in 1922, and possesses over 287,000 items, including the largest numismatic collection of Latin America. The architectural complex that houses the museum was built in 1603 as the St. James of Mercy Fort; earlier structures date back to 1567, erected by order of King Sebastian I of Portugal.","wpu":"National Historical Museum (Brazil)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Museu_Hist%C3%B3rico_Nacional_-_P%C3%A1tio_Gustavo_Barroso.jpg/960px-Museu_Hist%C3%B3rico_Nacional_-_P%C3%A1tio_Gustavo_Barroso.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Gustavo Capanema Palace","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-22.9092,"lo":-43.173828,"d":1837,"ds":"The Gustavo Capanema Palace (Portuguese: Palácio Gustavo Capanema), also known architecturally as the Ministry of Education and Health Building, is a government office building in downtown Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. As the first modernist project in Brazil, it is historically important to the architectural development of Modernism in the country and has been placed on Brazil's UNESCO tentative list.","wpu":"Gustavo Capanema Palace","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Vista_da_fachada_sul_do_Pal%C3%A1cio_Gustavo_Capanema.jpg/960px-Vista_da_fachada_sul_do_Pal%C3%A1cio_Gustavo_Capanema.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Rio de Janeiro, also known simply as Rio, is the capital and largest city of the state of Rio de Janeiro. It is the second-largest city in Brazil after São Paulo, and the largest by international tourists. It has a population of 6 million in the city proper, and 13 million people in its metropolitan area as of 2025.","wpu":"Rio de Janeiro","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Cidade_Maravilhosa.jpg/960px-Cidade_Maravilhosa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}